From afbe1e19c9d8926925579a0472fecb97a97cdb36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Robin Candau <robincandau@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 00:11:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Don't compress man page in during build
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Man pages compression is a distribution and/or user preference that should be left to the downstream packaging.

Compressing man pages by default imposes a compression algorithm and level while most distributions package managers handle that already (including eventual downstream preferences / expectations or policies) and also eventually allow users customization on that front.

Additionally, it can lead to unexpected side effects. For instance, `gzip` is recording timestamps in metadata by default, which prevents [reproducible builds](https://reproducible-builds.org/):

```
